slstatus - suckless status
==========================
slstatus is a suckless status monitor for window managers that use WM_NAME
(e.g. dwm) or stdin to fill the status bar.


Features
--------
- Battery percentage/power/state
- CPU usage
- CPU frequency
- CPU iowait
- Custom shell commands
- Date and time
- Disk status (free storage, percentage, total storage and used storage)
- Available entropy
- Username/GID/UID
- Hostname
- IP address (IPv4 and IPv6)
- Kernel version
- Keyboard indicators
- Load average
- Number of files in a directory (hint: Maildir)
- Memory status (free memory, percentage, total memory and used memory)
- Swap status (free swap, percentage, total swap and used swap)
- Temperature
- Uptime
- Volume percentage (OSS/ALSA)
- WiFi signal percentage and ESSID
- Razer Basilisk X HyperSpeed DPI and battery status
- AI usage popup powered by CodexBar CLI
- Herdr agent states with an animated attention indicator
- Click controls for audio devices, volume, microphone, and mouse DPI when used
  with the clickable-status patch in this dwm configuration


Status clicks
-------------
- Volume: left click selects the output device, middle click toggles mute, and
  the wheel adjusts output volume by 5% (clamped to 0-100%)
- Microphone: left click selects the input device, middle click toggles mute,
  and the wheel adjusts input volume by 5% (clamped to 0-100%)
- Mouse DPI: left click adds 100 DPI, right click subtracts 100 DPI (clamped
  to 100-16000 DPI)
- Date: left click shows a three-month calendar in a small `st` popup

The audio selectors embed a vertical dmenu list in the same top-right `st`
popup used by the other on-demand status views. Up to ten devices are visible
at once; dmenu filtering and keyboard navigation continue to work normally.
Only one selector can be open at a time. It closes after a selection, on
Escape, or when the pointer leaves the popup.

The mouse component writes the active DPI and its single DPI stage together,
so the physical DPI button remains locked to the displayed value.


Requirements
------------
In order to build slstatus you need the Xlib header files and optionally ALSA
for volume percentage. PulseAudio is not supported for various reasons.

- I needed `pulseaudio` and `pusleaudio-alsa` in order to get my headphone volume controls working.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (slstatus is installed into the
/usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install slstatus (if
necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running slstatus
----------------
See the man page for details.


Configuration
-------------
slstatus can be customized by creating a custom config.h and (re)compiling the
source code. This keeps it fast, secure and simple.


Herdr agent status
------------------
The Herdr component reads the local Unix socket API directly and shows a count
for each agent state: `!` blocked, `` done, `…` working, `○` idle, and `?`
unknown. Zero counts are omitted.

Blocked and done agents need attention. While either state is present, its `!`
or `` icon blinks on and off on each status refresh. The count remains visible.
The hidden `!` uses a normal space. The hidden `` uses the Nerd Font's empty
`U+EC03` glyph. Each blank matches its visible icon width, so the rest of the
status does not move. The `○N` idle/parked count remains steadily visible. For
example:

    Herdr !1 1 …2 ○1
    Herdr  1  1 …2 ○1

The component hides itself when Herdr is unavailable or has no detected
agents. Pass a socket path in `config.h` to override discovery. With a `NULL`
argument it checks `HERDR_SOCKET_PATH`, then
`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/herdr/herdr.sock`, or finally
`$HOME/.config/herdr/herdr.sock`.


AI usage
--------
Install the [CodexBar Linux CLI](https://github.com/steipete/CodexBar#cli-tarballs-macoslinux)
and make `codexbar` available on your desktop session's `PATH`. Configure the
providers you want with `codexbar config enable --provider <id>` and sign in
using each provider's supported source. For example, the provider IDs for the
previous four-row report are `claude`, `codex`, `grok`, and `opencodego`.
OpenCode Go needs the Go API key from the OpenCode Console. Signing in to
OpenCode v2 with Console OAuth alone does not provide that key to CodexBar.
Store the Go key with `codexbar config set-api-key --provider opencodego --stdin`
or use `OPENCODE_API_KEY`; then check it with
`codexbar usage --provider opencodego --source api`.

On Arch Linux, install the CLI with `yay -S codexbar-cli`.

Left-click the `󰚩` agent segment to open CodexBar's full cards in an `st`
popup. The bar cells use solid `█` for remaining capacity and shaded `░` for
used capacity, making the filled and empty portions distinct. OpenCode Go's
card includes its five-hour, weekly, and monthly windows. The popup closes
when it loses focus. Run `slstatus -u` to print the same report in a terminal.
CodexBar fetches usage when the popup opens; slstatus only changes the bar
glyphs and does not poll providers in the status loop.

Left-click the date to show `cal -3` in the same type of popup. DWM places both
popups at the top right before their first visible frame.
